#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/oauth-cli-tutorial/en.mdx:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this tutorial, you'll build a minimal Node.js tool that authenticates a user with [atproto OAuth](/guides/auth) and fetches their profile. The entire app fits in a single TypeScript file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/oauth-cli-tutorial/en.mdx:18
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is a good starting point for understanding how OAuth works in AT Protocol. For building production web apps, see the full [OAuth with NextJS Tutorial](/guides/oauth-tutorial).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/oauth-cli-tutorial/en.mdx:35
#, no-wrap
msgid "Create a new project directory and install the [SDK](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@atproto/lex) and other project dependencies:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/oauth-cli-tutorial/en.mdx:39
#, no-wrap
msgid "You'll also need the `lex` CLI tool and `tsx` installed globally, and the `app.bsky.actor.profile` Lexicon installed in this project to fetch the user's profile:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/oauth-cli-tutorial/en.mdx:43
#, no-wrap
msgid "This generates TypeScript types for the Lexicon in your project, which you'll use later to read the authenticated user's profile.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/oauth-cli-tutorial/en.mdx:47
#, no-wrap
msgid "Create a new file at `src/index.ts`. This file will contain all of the code for your CLI tool.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/oauth-cli-tutorial/en.mdx:49
#, no-wrap
msgid "Start by importing the packages you'll need, and setting up the OAuth client:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/oauth-cli-tutorial/en.mdx:53
#, no-wrap
msgid "The `NodeOAuthClient` manages the OAuth flow. `buildAtprotoLoopbackClientMetadata` configures it for local development using loopback.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/oauth-cli-tutorial/en.mdx:59
#, no-wrap
msgid "The `stateStore` and `sessionStore` are in-memory Maps. The state store holds temporary data during the OAuth handshake; the session store holds the authenticated session keyed by DID. In a production app, you'd use a database for these.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/oauth-cli-tutorial/en.mdx:63
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now add the login flow. You'll start a temporary HTTP server to receive the OAuth callback, then open the user's browser to authorize the app:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/oauth-cli-tutorial/en.mdx:72
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"1. `oauthClient.authorize(handle)` resolves the user's handle, discovers their PDS, and returns an authorization URL.\n"
"2. The `open` package opens that URL in the user's browser, where they'll see a consent screen.\n"
"3. After the user approves, their PDS redirects back to `http://127.0.0.1:3000/callback` with an authorization code.\n"
"4. `oauthClient.callback(params)` exchanges that code for an authenticated session.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/oauth-cli-tutorial/en.mdx:76
#, no-wrap
msgid "With the session in hand, you can create a lex `Client` to interact with the AT Protocol. Add a `main()` function that ties it all together:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/oauth-cli-tutorial/en.mdx:80
#, no-wrap
msgid "The `Client` from `@atproto/lex` accepts the OAuth session directly — the same way it accepts a `PasswordSession` in the [Build an Agent tutorial](/guides/bot-tutorial). From here you can `.get()`, `.list()`, `.create()`, and `.call()` any installed Lexicon.\n"
msgstr ""

